92186045417] [17592186049824 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049824 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049824 #edb/ref 1031 "Logical Associative and Commutative Laws" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049824 #edb/ref 1033 2830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049824 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "16d9828c-8d8f-5f94-9530-7dc3e79040cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050747 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050747 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050747 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050747 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186198256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050747 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186198257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050747 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186198258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050747 #edb/ref 1031 "Simplifying Predicate Expressions Using De Morgan's Laws" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050747 #edb/ref 1033 4302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050747 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "6358ff23-2fc9-5b14-8d25-ca07cea2fd02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194760 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186194761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194760 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186194762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194760 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186194763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194760 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186194764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194760 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186194765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194760 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194760 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194760 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bb53ef75-3c2a-5730-a062-a4208ae377f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194761 #edb/ref 1080 "P∧Q" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194761 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194761 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "822e6a30-0fa6-5438-9aa4-df4efa9a1785" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194762 #edb/ref 1080 "P∨Q" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194762 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194762 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "92f13186-7ef2-56a6-afaa-ede0ce2ed159" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194763 #edb/ref 1080 "P∨(P∧Q)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194763 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194763 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9cab27f4-f8c8-5435-ac8b-25cb574b6b71" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194764 #edb/ref 1080 "P∧P" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194764 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194764 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b2a7869d-8b6d-53eb-b4a4-fc00d63915d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194765 #edb/ref 1080 "P∧(Q∨P)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194765 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194765 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "629b3ce3-6280-547d-8a34-4dc84203c7a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198099 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12724" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198099 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following compound predicates:\n\n$x$ is even **and** divisible by $5.$ \n\n$x$ is even **or** less than $6$.\n\nExpress these compound predicates above in symbolic form given the following notations:\n\n- $A(x):$ $x$ is even\n\n- $B(x):$ $x$ is divisible by $5$\n\n- $C(x):$ $x$ is less than $6$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198099 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d40cb0df-fce7-5236-b0ef-62f5a343b3a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198099 #edb/ref 1074 "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$\\land.$\" \n\nSo, the first sentence reads\n\n![](images/6e/6e906074c9243c4189238c6ef5606954422bb7b8d2d3ad0b373579d25e221bb7.png)\n\nthat is, $A \\land B.$\n\nMoreover, the word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$\\lor.$\" \n\nSo, the second sentence reads\n\n![](images/e5/e557113d18858d98a329fe4ce3ba84754defdc652095ccf57844077b7426d154.png)\n\nthat is, $A \\lor C.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198100 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12725" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198100 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following compound predicate:\n\n$x$ is positive **and** a multiple of $4,$ **or** $x$ is negative.\n\nExpress this compound predicate in symbolic form given the following notations:\n\n- $P(x):$ $x$ is positive\n\n- $M(x):$ $x$ is a multiple of $4$\n\n- $N(x):$ $x$ is negative" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198100 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3e2319b4-ab10-50ef-845b-9586c9761227" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198100 #edb/ref 1074 "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$\\land,$\" while the word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$\\lor.$\"\n\nNote that this sentence consists of two parts:\n\n- **Part 1:** $x$ is positive and a multiple of $4.$ This corresponds to\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x \\,\\textrm{is positive}}_P \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{and}}_{\\land} \\quad \\underbrace{x \\vphantom{p}\\textrm{ is a multiple of }4 }_M,\n$$\n\nthat is, $P \\land M.$\n\n- **Part 2:** $x$ is negative. This corresponds to $N.$\n\nSo, we can express the sentence as\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x \\,\\textrm{ is positive}\\,\\textrm{and a multiple of }4 }_{P \\land M} \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{or}}_{\\lor} \\quad \\underbrace{x \\vphantom{p}\\textrm{ is negative} }_N,\n$$\n\nthat is, $(P \\land M) \\lor N.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198101 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12726" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198101 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the truth set of $P(x) \\lor Q(x)$ given the following predicates defined over the universal set $\\mathbb{R}{:}$\n\n- $P(x):$ $3x -1 < 5$\n\n- $Q(x):$ $x \\geq 3$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198101 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3a115dd5-3241-5627-bb40-121b75eeb7ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198101 #edb/ref 1074 "The truth set of $P(x) \\lor Q(x)$ represents the *union* of the truth sets of $P(x)$ and $Q(x){:}$\n\n$$\nT_{P \\lor Q} = T_P \\cup T_Q\n$$\n\nWe start by finding the truth sets of $P(x)$ and $Q(x){:}$\n\n- To find the the truth set of $P(x),$ we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-1 & <5 \\\\ 3x & <6 \\\\ x & <2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, the truth set of $P(x)$ is\n$T_P = (-\\infty,2).$\n\n- The truth set of $Q(x)$ is \n$T_Q = [3,\\infty).$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P∨Q} & ={T}_{P}\\cup {T}_{Q} \\\\  & =(-\\infty ,2)\\cup [3,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198104 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12733" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198104 #edb/ref 1071 "Write a sentence that is equivalent to the following: \"$x$ is an integer, and $x$ is negative and it is an integer.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198104 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2ab89571-f4e2-515f-9d01-b1213e73ffc0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198104 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the commutative law, we can replace the phrase \"$x$ is negative and it is an integer\" with the phrase \"$x$ is an integer and it is negative.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"$x$ is an integer, and $x$ is an integer and it is negative.\"\n\nThen, using the associative law, we can move the comma:\n\n\"$x$ is an integer and $x$ is an integer, and it is negative.\"\n\nNow, we can simplify the phrase \"$x$ is an integer and $x$ is an integer\" to just \"$x$ is an integer\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"$x$ is an integer, and it is negative.\"\n\nFinally, we can write the sentence more concisely:\n\n\"$x$ is a negative integer.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198252 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198252 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198252 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198252 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186381368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198252 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186381495 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198252 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198252 #edb/ref 1039 "Expressing a Compound Predicate in Symbolic Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198252 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186198099 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198252 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2993b4ab-e45b-579a-8a11-b5e9ee107d5b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198253 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198253 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198253 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186381375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198253 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186381382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198253 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186381502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198253 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389670 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198253 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198253 #edb/ref 1039 "Expressing a Nested Compound Predicate in Symbolic Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198253 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186198100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198253 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "ce659c55-ca26-52c6-b15e-ec317013d856" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198254 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198254 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198254 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198254 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186381410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198254 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186381551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198254 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186381579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198254 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198254 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198254 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding Truth Sets of Conjunctions and Disjunctions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198254 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186198101 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198254 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e6bc626a-9517-584c-bf55-8653227f830e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198255 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198255 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198255 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198255 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186381389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198255 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186381396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198255 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186381403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198255 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198255 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198255 #edb/ref 1039 "Using Laws Involving Conjunction and Disjunction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198255 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186198104 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198255 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "5f674fcf-7f3e-53c0-8759-6f83b0820160" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198514 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198514 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198514 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198514 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199042 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198514 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198514 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198514 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198514 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198514 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198514 #edb/ref 1045 "The \"And\" and \"Or\" Connectives With Predicates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198514 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186199039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198514 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198514 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "f9f9eac5-9750-5e10-9a18-8a361f45282b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198689 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198689 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186381362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198689 #edb/ref 1069 "q-170725" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198689 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198689 #edb/ref 1071 "$x$ is even **or** odd.\n\nExpress the compound predicate above in symbolic form given the following notations:\n\n- $P(x):$ $x$ is even\n\n- $Q(x):$ $x$ is odd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198689 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7ff7b2b7-dfb5-55b1-bfd4-172f42c8239c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198689 #edb/ref 1074 "The word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$∨$\".\n\nSo, the sentence reads\n\n$$\n{{x\\text{ is even}}_{⏟}}_{P}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{x\\text{ is odd}}_{⏟}}_{Q},\n$$\n\nthat is, $P∨Q.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199039 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199039 #edb/ref 1061 120115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199039 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b041ca76-9538-556f-ba42-756566d8edf6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199039 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199040 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199040 #edb/ref 1061 120116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199040 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8701928d-1944-5363-b962-b4891f892daa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199040 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199041 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199042 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199041 #edb/ref 1061 120119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199041 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "77ba67c1-cc43-57be-ac98-62a769bb7e42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199041 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199042 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199042 #edb/ref 1061 120122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199042 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ea6a5438-89a7-508e-92fb-e311fd2c5c48" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199042 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199043 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199043 #edb/ref 1061 120123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199043 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9905e635-bc99-5ab6-8557-95a009250e4e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199043 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199044 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199044 #edb/ref 1061 120124 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199044 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "69f763bb-fe3d-5367-b28c-7de5d1163729" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199044 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199045 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199045 #edb/ref 1061 120128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199045 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "71a8a715-2397-5052-a148-b7cc79853a2b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199045 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199046 #edb/ref 1061 120129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199046 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1887f6f2-227f-5082-89ee-2e84c56eb994" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199046 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199520 #edb/ref 1064 "We can apply the logical operations of conjunction and disjunction to predicates.\n\n- The **conjunction** of the predicates $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(y_1,y_2,\\ldots,y_m)$ is a new predicate which we denote as follows: $$P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n) \\land Q(y_1,y_2,\\ldots,y_m)$$ This conjunction is true whenever $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ is true *and* $Q(y_1,y_2,\\ldots,y_m)$ is true for a specific set of respective variable assignments. The conjunction is false if either $P$ or $Q$ is false.\n- The **disjunction** of the predicates $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(y_1,y_2,\\ldots,y_m)$ is written as follows: $$P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n) \\lor Q(y_1,y_2,\\ldots,y_m)$$ This disjunction is true whenever $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ is true, $Q(y_1,y_2,\\ldots,y_m)$ is true, or both are true, for a specific set of variable assignments. The disjunction is false if $P$ *and* $Q$ are false.\n\nFor example, consider the following predicates:\n\n- $P(n):$ $n$ is even\n- $Q(n):$ $n$ is positive\n\nThen, $P(n) \\land Q(n)$ is a new predicate that reads \"$n$ is even *and* $n$ is positive,\" which can be simplified to \"$n$ is even and positive.\"\n\nOn the other hand, $P(n) \\lor Q(n)$ is a new predicate that reads \"$n$ is even *or* $n$ is positive,\" which can be simplified to \"$n$ is even or positive.\"\n\nWhen it's clear that $P(n)$ is a predicate in the variable $n,$ we sometimes omit the variables when writing them. So, $P(n) \\land Q(n)$ and $P(n) \\lor Q(n)$ can be written as $P \\land Q$ and $P \\lor Q,$ respectively." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199520 #edb/ref 1065 7984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199520 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c05b0e9a-b514-5c42-9d07-7ec2466b30be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199520 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199521 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $T_P$ and $T_Q$ be the truth sets of the predicates $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n),$ respectively. Note that we're assuming our predicates have the same universal set.\n\nFrom the definition of the conjunction of predicates, we have that $P \\land Q$ is true if both $P$ and $Q$ are true. Therefore, the truth set of $P \\land Q$ is the *intersection* of the truth sets of $P$ and $Q{:}$\n\n$$T_{P \\land Q} = T_P \\cap T_Q$$\n\nFor example, consider the following predicates defined over the set of positive integers:\n\n- $P(x):$ $x$ is divisible by $3$\n- $Q(x):$ $24$ is divisible by $x$\n\nWe start by finding the truth sets of $P(x)$ and $Q(x){:}$\n\n- The truth set of $P(x)$ is $$T_P = \\big\\{ 3,6,9,12,15,18,21,24,27 \\ldots \\big\\}.$$\n- The truth set of $Q(x)$ is $$T_Q = \\big\\{ 1,2,3,4,6,8,12,24 \\big\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P∧Q} & ={T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q} \\\\ & ={3,6,9,12,15,18,21,24,27…}\\cap {1,2,3,4,6,8,12,24} \\\\ & ={3,6,12,24}.\\end{aligned}$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199521 #edb/ref 1065 7985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199521 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "74eb89b1-6654-56b5-9fef-718a3acfcb2f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199521 #edb/ref 1067 "The Truth Set of a Conjunction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199523 #edb/ref 1064 "Conjunction and disjunction of predicates are generalizations of logical operations on statements. Moreover, the properties of these operations on statements can be translated to corresponding operations on predicates.\n\nIn particular, the idempotent, absorption, associative, and commutative laws also apply to conjunction and disjunction of predicates.\n\nSo, for predicates $P(x_1,x_2\\ldots,x_n),$ $Q(y_1,y_2\\ldots,y_m),$ and $R(z_1,z_2\\ldots,z_p),$ we have the following:\n\n- *The Idempotent Laws*: $$\\begin{aligned}P∧P≡P∨P≡P\\end{aligned}$$\n- *The Associative Laws*: $$\\begin{aligned}P∧(Q∧R) & ≡(P∧Q)∧R \\\\ P∨(Q∨R) & ≡(P∨Q)∨R\\end{aligned}$$\n- *The Commutative Laws*: $$\\begin{aligned}P∧Q & ≡Q∧P \\\\ P∨Q & ≡Q∨P\\end{aligned}$$\n- *The Distributive Laws*: $$\\begin{aligned}P∧(Q∨R) & ≡(P∧Q)∨(P∧R) \\\\ P∨(Q∧R) & ≡(P∨Q)∧(P∨R)\\end{aligned}$$\n- *The Absorption Laws*: $$\\begin{aligned}P∧(P∨Q) & ≡P \\\\ P∨(P∧Q) & ≡P\\end{aligned}$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199523 #edb/ref 1065 7989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199523 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6ad96982-4e36-52f9-816a-db64ba277433" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199523 #edb/ref 1067 "Laws Involving Conjunction and Disjunction of Predicates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199542 #edb/ref 1064 "Once again, let $T_P$ and $T_Q$ be the truth sets of the predicates $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n),$ respectively.\n\nFrom the definition of the disjunction of predicates, we have that $P \\lor Q$ is true if $P$ is true, or $Q$ is true, or both are true. Therefore, the truth set of $P \\lor Q$ must be the *union* of the truth sets of $P$ and $Q{:}$\n\n$$T_{P \\lor Q} = T_P \\cup T_Q$$\n\nFor example, suppose we are given the following shaded regions that represent the truth sets of the predicates $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y),$ defined over the universal set $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\n![](images/ce/ce71e62503acd1c82f6bba7e1d9c83c40f0a98f04c689c3ccd58e7a99a814856.png)\n\nTo get the truth set of $P(x,y) \\lor Q(x,y),$ we take all the points that belong to $T_P$ or $T_Q,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/9c/9c528c5fe390bd1f22cb473c01b768e83f5b3a35d97eb7e670c6734b8cb2c2f8.png)\n\n**Note:** The points where the *dashed* line meets the *solid* curve belong to $T_P \\cup T_Q$ since these points lie in $T_P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199542 #edb/ref 1065 8140 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199542 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4d1b797a-ff89-514d-a0df-87d6858b45e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199542 #edb/ref 1067 "The Truth Set of a Disjunction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381362 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381362 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381362 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381362 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381362 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381362 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381362 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381362 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186381367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381362 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b22e7a73-f4cf-5a91-83ba-e94d101da87b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381363 #edb/ref 1080 "P∧P" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381363 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381363 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "41dde3e3-6092-50d7-a3e3-9b03129714b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381364 #edb/ref 1080 "P∨(P∧Q)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381364 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381364 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ab38e2e3-7aa0-5681-bd79-b716a1f5ebfb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381365 #edb/ref 1080 "P∧Q" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381365 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381365 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "765bffbf-d84a-5ff5-a5f8-870a92ad58fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381366 #edb/ref 1080 "P∧(Q∨P)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381366 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381366 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "63cf5e8f-593a-5960-b49a-27fd32e7eff6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381367 #edb/ref 1080 "P∨Q" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381367 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381367 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1b83a791-8241-5cd5-855b-476449665c17" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381368 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186381369 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381368 #edb/ref 1069 "q-170735" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381368 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381368 #edb/ref 1071 "$x$ is rational  **or** greater than $10.$\n\nExpress the compound predicate above in symbolic form given the following notations:\n\n- $A(x):$ $x$ is rational\n\n- $B(x):$ $x$ is greater than $10$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381368 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "694a195b-decb-5476-962c-92767f80b122" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381368 #edb/ref 1074 "The word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$∨.$\"\n\nSo, the sentence reads\n\n$$\n{{x\\text{ is rational}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{x>10}_{⏟}}_{B},\n$$\n\nthat is, $A∨B.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381369 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381369 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381369 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381369 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381369 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381369 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381369 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381369 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186381371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381369 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cc686a5e-3a2b-5cc8-ad6d-1246bf1f5079" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381370 #edb/ref 1080 "A∨(A∧B)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381370 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381370 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "443c2ac2-d73e-5209-b21f-beb333bf7d18" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381371 #edb/ref 1080 "A∨B" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381371 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381371 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ea606823-2fa9-5cc2-990c-5045edd76dfc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381372 #edb/ref 1080 "A∧(B∨A)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381372 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381372 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8c7ab119-0282-5fbe-9bf9-0e338586bd6a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381373 #edb/ref 1080 "A∧B" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381373 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381373 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8d3b63ed-53f9-5121-b506-5b94b2f06ab5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381374 #edb/ref 1080 "B∧B" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381374 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381374 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "baa4f7d6-0324-5b7e-8071-793d1a8bec13" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381502 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186381503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381502 #edb/ref 1069 "q-175377" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381502 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381502 #edb/ref 1071 "$x$ is negative **or** rational, **and** $x$ is positive **or** an  integer.\n\nExpress the compound predicate above in symbolic form given the following notations:\n\n- $N(x):$ $x$ is negative\n\n- $R(x):$ $x$ is rational\n\n- $P(x):$ $x$ is positive\n\n- $I(x):$ $x$ is an integer" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381502 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "357fd90c-bc44-5b21-9839-d55874391b78" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381502 #edb/ref 1074 "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$∧$,\" while the word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$∨.$\"\n\nNote that this sentence consists of two parts:\n\n- **Part 1:** $x$ is negative **or** rational. This corresponds to\n\n$$\n{{x\\text{ is negative }}_{⏟}}_{N}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{x\\text{ is rational }}_{⏟}}_{R},\n$$\n\nthat is, $N∨R.$\n\n- **Part 2:** $x$ is positive **or** an integer. This corresponds to\n\n$$\n{{x\\text{ is positive }}_{⏟}}_{P}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{x\\text{ is an integer }}_{⏟}}_{I},\n$$\n\nthat is, $P∨I.$\n\nSo, we can express the sentence as\n\n$$\n{{x\\text{ is negative or rational}}_{⏟}}_{N\\,∨\\,R}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{x\\text{ is positive or an integer }}_{⏟}}_{P\\,∨\\,I},\n$$\n\nthat is, $(N∨R)∧(P∨I).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381503 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381503 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381503 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381503 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381503 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381503 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381503 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381503 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186381505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381503 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "33ea1fa2-4f60-58ac-aee0-0a9d809fa209" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381504 #edb/ref 1080 "(N∨R)∨(P∨I)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381504 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381504 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d99eb173-3abf-5775-a859-5a92ae2ae01f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381505 #edb/ref 1080 "(N∨R)∧(P∨I)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381505 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381505 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "df7b7424-fa58-5870-87bc-c682e2678be0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381506 #edb/ref 1080 "((N∨R)∧P)∨I" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381506 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381506 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "28e544d4-09ee-5450-aac7-cedff79d4967" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381507 #edb/ref 1080 "(N∧R)∧(P∧I)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381507 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381507 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d99f5091-2095-5600-a488-bf4114dc1018" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381508 #edb/ref 1080 "(N∧R)∨(P∧I)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381508 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381508 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6dd81fe8-96cc-5534-bfef-3c16765feb7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389790 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186389791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389790 #edb/ref 1069 "q-177645" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389790 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389790 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/76/761e02a7613e2d1df4722b7bf6f0a7e1d0b5e7b6dec09d205a1e72a29b8c5eb8.png)\n\nThe shaded regions above represent the truth sets of the predicates $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y),$ defined over the universal set ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following is the truth set of $P∨Q?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389790 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4eda50f7-a585-5a86-9e62-dbc7c5c300f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389790 #edb/ref 1074 "The truth set of $P∨Q$ represents th